# File: tests/bdd/features/audio_finishing.feature
# Generated from: specs/252-trim-cut-loudness-match-and-loop-crossfade-sounds-when-import-audio-imports-them/requirements.md
Feature: Finish imported sounds

  @SCN001
  Scenario: Leading silence is trimmed
    Given a sound effect WAV that starts with 400 ms of silence before a tone
    When import_audio imports it as "Chime" with default settings
    Then the game copy starts within 10 ms of the tone
    And SourceArt/Audio/SFX/Chime.wav is byte-identical to the input

  @SCN002
  Scenario: A sound is cut to a maximum length
    Given a 4-second tone WAV
    When import_audio imports it as "Whoosh" with max_seconds 1.5
    Then the game copy is 1.5 s long within 10 ms
    And its last 20 ms fade to zero

  @SCN003
  Scenario Outline: Loudness matches the target
    Given a tone WAV measuring <input> LUFS
    When import_audio imports it as kind "<kind>" with default settings
    Then the finished audio measures <target> LUFS within 1 LU

    Examples:
      | input | kind  | target |
      | -30   | sfx   | -16    |
      | -6    | sfx   | -16    |
      | -30   | music | -18    |

  @SCN004
  Scenario: Loops are crossfaded
    Given a tone WAV whose last sample does not match its first
    When import_audio imports it with loop true
    Then the step between the finished audio's last and first sample is no larger than its largest internal step

  @SCN005
  Scenario: Finishing is repeatable
    Given "Chime" was imported with max_seconds 1.0
    When the same file is imported again as "Chime" with no settings, and then refinish --all runs
    Then each game copy is byte-identical to the first one
    And SourceArt/Audio/audio.json records max_seconds 1.0 for "Chime"
    And importing a different file as "Chime" fails with "audio.asset_exists"

  @SCN006
  Scenario: Missing ffmpeg is reported
    Given ffmpeg is not on PATH and FFMPEG_BIN is unset
    When import_audio imports a music file
    Then it fails with "audio.ffmpeg_not_found" and a remediation line and no game copy exists
    And a sound-effect import still succeeds

  @SCN007
  Scenario: Finishing can be turned off per sound
    Given a sound effect WAV that starts with silence
    When import_audio imports it with finish false
    Then the game copy is byte-identical to the stored WAV
    And SourceArt/Audio/audio.json records finish false
